.TH VIRCON32 3v32 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 DevTools 26.04.24" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
vircon32 \- the Vircon32 C standard library and console programming model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqvideo.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqinput.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqaudio.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqtime.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqmath.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqstring.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqmisc.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqmemcard.h\(dq
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Vircon32 C compiler,
.BR compile (1),
comes with eight library headers. They are installed in the
.I include
directory next to the compiler
.RI ( /usr/local/Vircon32/DevTools/include
by default on Linux and macOS), where
.B #include \(dq...\(dq
finds them. Only the quoted form of
.B #include
exists.
.PP
There is no separate library to link: each header contains the full
definitions of its functions, and the compiler emits only the ones a
program calls. Most functions are a few lines of inline assembly that
read or write one of the console's I/O ports, so they cost a call and
a handful of cycles.
.TP
.BR video.h (3v32)
The GPU: textures, regions, drawing, colors, blending, text with the
BIOS font.
.TP
.BR input.h (3v32)
The gamepads: directions and buttons.
.TP
.BR audio.h (3v32)
The SPU: sounds, channels, playback and volume.
.TP
.BR time.h (3v32)
The timer: frames, the clock and date, waiting for the next frame.
.TP
.BR math.h (3v32)
Arithmetic, rounding, trigonometry and exponentials on the CPU's own
instructions.
.TP
.BR string.h (3v32)
Character classification and conversion, strings, and conversion of
numbers to text.
.TP
.BR misc.h (3v32)
Memory copying and filling, dynamic memory, random numbers, halting.
.TP
.BR memcard.h (3v32)
The memory card: game signatures, reading and writing saved data.
.SH THE PROGRAMMING MODEL
.SS Words
The CPU addresses memory in 32-bit words, and every scalar C type
.RB ( int ,
.BR float ,
.BR bool ,
pointers, and
.BR char ,
which is an
.BR int )
is one word.
.B sizeof
counts words, and so do all the sizes taken by this library
.RB ( memcpy ,
.BR malloc ,
.BR card_read_data ,
\&...). A string is an array of words, one character per word,
ending with 0; the library declares strings as
.BR int* .
.SS Frames
The console runs at 60 frames per second, and the CPU executes 250000
instructions per frame (15 million per second), one per cycle. A game's
main loop does one frame of work and then calls
.BR end_frame (3v32),
which waits for the next frame to begin; gamepad state is updated
between frames. The screen is not cleared automatically: what was drawn
stays until it is drawn over.
.SS Selection
The GPU and SPU are programmed through \(lqselected\(rq objects: a
texture and a region within it, a sound, a channel; the input
controller likewise has a selected gamepad. Many functions act on
whatever is currently selected
.RB ( set_channel_volume ,
.BR define_region ,
.BR gamepad_button_a ,
\&...), and some change the selection as a side effect (the
.B *_channel
functions that take a channel id,
.BR play_sound ,
.BR draw_region_at
through the drawing point,
.BR print_at
through the region). Each header's page says which.
.SS Out-of-range values
Writing an invalid value to a port (a texture or sound id that does not
exist, a channel outside 0\-15) is ignored and leaves the previous
value. Numeric parameters are clamped to their range (drawing points to
\-1000 .. screen size + 1000, scale factors to \-1024 .. 1024, region
corners to 0 .. 1023, channel volume to 0 .. 8, channel speed to
0 .. 128, global volume to 0 .. 2).
.SS GPU capacity
In each frame the GPU can draw at most 9 times the pixels of the
screen. Clearing the screen costs half a screen; a region costs its
on-screen area (limited to the screen size), times 1.15 when zoomed,
1.25 when rotated, or 1.4 when both. A request that does not fit in
what is left of the frame is dropped, and so is every other drawing
request until the next frame.
.SS Hardware errors
Division or remainder by zero (integer or float), an arc cosine,
arc tangent, logarithm or power outside its domain, a stack overflow or
underflow,
and reading or writing an address or port that does not exist stop the
program: the CPU jumps to the BIOS, which shows an error screen with
the error and the instruction address. The library's comments mark
the functions that can do this.
.SS The null pointer
.B NULL
is a keyword of Vircon32 C and its value is \-1, not 0.
.B malloc
(see
.BR misc.h (3v32))
returns
.B NULL
(\-1) when it fails.
.SH MEMORY MAP
.TS
l l l.
0x00000000	RAM	4194304 words
0x10000000	BIOS program ROM	up to 1048576 words
0x20000000	cartridge program ROM	up to 134217728 words
0x30000000	memory card	262144 words
.TE
.PP
Global variables are placed at the start of RAM and the stack starts at
the top, growing down;
.B malloc
(see
.BR misc.h (3v32))
by default uses the middle half.
.SH I/O PORTS
Ports are read with the
.B IN
instruction and written with
.BR OUT ;
see
.BR assemble (1)
for their names.
.TS
l l.
0x000	timer (TIM_*)
0x100	random number generator (RNG_*)
0x200	GPU (GPU_*)
0x300	SPU (SPU_*)
0x400	gamepads (INP_*)
0x500	cartridge (CAR_*)
0x600	memory card (MEM_*)
.TE
.SH NOTES
Several functions have the same names as standard C functions
.RB ( memcpy ,
.BR malloc ,
.BR strlen ,
.BR sin ,
.BR sleep ,
\&...) but not always the same behavior; their pages say where they
differ. On a system with the standard C manual pages installed, ask for
this library's page explicitly:
.B man 3v32 string.h
or
.BR "man 3v32 strcpy" .
.PP
The SDK headers can also be used from C++ through
.BR v32c++ (1),
which passes their
.B #include
lines through to the C compiler; see
.BR v32c++\-headers (3v32)
for the C++ wrappers built on them.
.SH EXAMPLES
A complete program that moves a square with the D-pad:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "input.h"
#include "time.h"

void main()
{
    int x = 320;
    int y = 180;
    int dx;
    int dy;

    select_gamepad( 0 );
    select_texture( \-1 );          // the BIOS texture
    select_region( 20 );           // a solid block character

    while( true )
    {
        gamepad_direction( &dx, &dy );
        x += 2 * dx;
        y += 2 * dy;

        clear_screen( color_black );
        set_multiply_color( color_green );
        draw_region_at( x, y );
        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR compile (1),
.BR assemble (1),
.BR packrom (1),
.BR Vircon32 (1),
.BR audio.h (3v32),
.BR input.h (3v32),
.BR math.h (3v32),
.BR memcard.h (3v32),
.BR misc.h (3v32),
.BR string.h (3v32),
.BR time.h (3v32),
.BR video.h (3v32)
.PP
The Vircon32 specification and tutorials:
.I https://www.vircon32.com
.SH AUTHOR
The Vircon32 standard library was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
This manual page was written by Matthew Haas for the v32c++ project.
